# Force LF on everything that a Linux server executes or parses.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is set on the Windows dev machine, so the working
# copy of every text file has CRLF even though the committed blob is LF
# (`git ls-files --eol` shows `i/lf w/crlf`). That is harmless until a file
# is copied to the server DIRECTLY FROM THE WORKING TREE with scp instead
# of being checked out there.
#
# On 20 Aug 2026 `deploy/cron-ping.sh` was about to be scp'd that way. With
# CRLF, `#!/bin/bash\r` fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M", and
# `BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000\r` puts a carriage return inside the URL
# curl is given. It would have broken all three cron endpoints, not just
# the new one, and the shebang error would have pointed at bash rather than
# at line endings.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working copy LF too, so the trap disappears whether a
# file arrives by checkout or by scp.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
deploy/crontab text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
